    "Postoperative development of pain, anemia, electrolyte imbalance, fluid shifts, and sepsis can contribute to new-onset POAF and a rapid ventricular response. Aggressive management of these triggers is essential in POAF management. Medications like beta blockers or CCBs can control ventricular rate, with digoxin as an adjunct. Patients with AF require specific therapies, and synchronized cardioversion may be needed for hemodynamically unstable patients. Excluding left atrial appendage thrombus is necessary before attempting rhythm control in specific cases. POAF after NCS is associated with thromboembolic risks. Strategies include using oral nonvitamin K anticoagulants or no anticoagulation in patients with NCS onset AF. Continuous monitoring in cardiac surgery patients can detect AF effectively. Outpatient follow-up is recommended within 3 to 6 months after NCS to evaluate AF presence. For patients with cardiovascular implantable electronic devices, an EMI management plan is essential before surgery. Reprogramming pacemakers may be necessary to avoid pacing inhibition.": "genes",
